## TL;DR — Asscher Cut Diamond Clarity

**Core finding:** Asscher cut diamond clarity matters more than in any other popular shape. The step-cut facets are flat mirrors — they reflect inclusions back and forth across the stone. An SI1 inclusion that disappears in a round brilliant becomes visible and often multiplied in an asscher.

**The minimum:** VS2 with photo verification. VS1 is the recommended starting grade for most buyers. At 2ct and above, VS1 is the floor — do not go lower.

**Best natural entry (VS1):** → [GIA 1.00ct F-VS1 at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

**Best lab entry (IF):** → [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — flawless clarity at near 1ct natural price

**Priority:** If you are comparing VS1 vs VVS2, spend the premium on size instead — VVS2 adds no visible benefit over VS1 in asscher. If you are comparing VS2 vs VS1, always go VS1. The step facets expose every flaw you chose to accept.

---

[Asscher cut diamond](/asscher-cut-diamond) clarity is the one specification that separates experienced buyers from first-timers. Every other quality factor — color, cut, carat — behaves roughly the same across diamond shapes. Clarity does not.

In the asscher, it behaves differently than in any other popular shape, and the difference is punishing.

The step-cut structure that produces the hall-of-mirrors effect also eliminates the optical cover that brilliant cuts provide. Round brilliants scatter light in thousands of directions — inclusions hide in that fire and scintillation. Asscher cut diamonds have long, flat, parallel facets that reflect light cleanly from one surface to another.

Every inclusion sits in full view inside those corridors.

This guide covers the exact clarity grades that work, the grades to avoid, why SI1 fails in asscher even when it passes in round, and what the price difference looks like between grades you should actually buy.

---

## Why Clarity Matters More in Asscher Than Any Other Cut

The asscher and the round brilliant are both 58-facet diamonds. The facets produce completely different optical effects, and those effects determine how much an inclusion affects what you see.

A round brilliant has kite-shaped, triangular, and star facets arranged to maximize light dispersion. Light enters, bounces between angled facets, and exits in many directions simultaneously. The result is fire and scintillation — constant visual movement that makes the surface of the diamond a shifting mosaic of brightness.

Inclusions inside a round brilliant are surrounded by that movement. They become part of the visual noise.

An asscher's step facets are flat, parallel, and arranged in long rows. Light enters and travels in controlled, predictable corridors before reflecting back. The result is the architectural hall-of-mirrors effect.

That same architecture means inclusions are framed by stillness, not movement. A small crystal sitting inside an asscher is not surrounded by competing sparkle — it sits in a clean, quiet mirror.

The difference in how inclusions present is not subtle. In controlled tests where the same SI1 stone is cut as both a round brilliant and an asscher, the round passes at arm's length while the asscher reveals the inclusion without any magnification tool. This is not a knock on the asscher cut — it is the geometry of step-cut optics.

The same feature that creates the deep corridor look also removes the optical camouflage that hides inclusions in brilliant cuts.

---

## Asscher Cut Diamond Clarity Chart — Grade by Grade

Here is how each clarity grade performs specifically in the asscher cut, not in diamonds generally.

| Clarity Grade | Visible in Asscher? | Eye Clean? | Recommended? | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FL / IF | Never visible | Always | Overkill | Zero inclusions by definition — worth it only for resale/insurance premium or emotional preference |
| VVS1 / VVS2 | Not visible even under 10× | Always | Optional upgrade | Eye-clean guaranteed; inclusions so small they cannot be found without magnification even by experts |
| VS1 | Visible under 10× only | Always | ✅ Recommended floor | Safest practical grade for asscher — clean to the naked eye in all lighting conditions |
| VS2 | Visible under 10×, occasionally borderline | Usually | ✅ With photo review | Can be eye-clean, but step facets expose some VS2 inclusions that would pass in round — requires verification |
| SI1 | Often visible to naked eye | Rarely | ❌ Avoid without expert review | Step facets amplify SI1 inclusions; what passes in round frequently fails in asscher |
| SI2 | Usually visible | Almost never | ❌ Not recommended | Inclusions visible face-up in asscher without magnification in most cases |
| I1 / I2 | Always visible | Never | ❌ Never | Never appropriate for asscher; inclusions will dominate the hall-of-mirrors view |

**What this means:** The clarity cutoff in asscher sits one full grade higher than in round brilliant for every practical buying decision. VS2 is the minimum where the round buyer stops, but it is where the asscher buyer needs to start — and only with photo review. VS1 is where you start with confidence in asscher without needing verification.

**Verdict:** For asscher cut diamonds, treat VS1 as your baseline, not your upgrade. The cost difference between VS2 and VS1 in asscher is not a luxury premium — it is the cost of not having to verify every stone individually. If your budget cannot reach VS1, buy VS2 only with magnified photos of the specific stone.

---

## The Mirror Multiplication Effect

Most buyers understand that step cuts "show inclusions more." What they do not understand is why — and how extreme the effect actually is.

Call it the Mirror Multiplication Effect. A step-cut facet is a flat, polished surface. When light enters the asscher and travels through the interior, it passes through the stone's step facets repeatedly before exiting.

Each reflection carries with it whatever is inside the stone — including inclusions.

A single crystal inclusion positioned at the center of the table in an asscher does not just appear once. It appears in the direct view. It appears again in the reflection from the opposite pavilion facet.

It appears in the secondary reflections from the step facets on each side. A single SI1 inclusion can appear as two, three, or even four distinct marks when viewed face-up, depending on its position and the stone's proportions.

This is the mechanism buyers miss when they compare GIA clarity certificates between shapes. The certificate describes what the inclusion is and where it sits — it does not describe how many times the step facets will reflect it. A "minor crystal near the culet, not affecting transparency" sounds harmless.

In a round brilliant, it is. In an asscher, the culet inclusion gets reflected in both the table view and the pavilion facets, doubling its visibility.

The Mirror Multiplication Effect is most severe in three positions: inclusions at the center of the table, inclusions in the pavilion directly beneath the table, and inclusions along the step facet edges. Inclusions near the girdle or under the bezel facets are less likely to multiply. This is why position matters as much as grade — and why a VS2 stone with an inclusion near the girdle can outperform an SI1 stone with an inclusion at the table center.

---

## Eye Clean Asscher Cut Diamonds — What Grade Achieves It?

"Eye clean" means you cannot see the inclusion without magnification at normal viewing distance (about 10–12 inches). In round brilliants, SI1 achieves eye clean about 60–70% of the time. In asscher cut diamonds, SI1 achieves eye clean roughly 20–30% of the time.

The grades that reliably produce eye-clean results in asscher:

**VS1 — Eye clean 99%+ of the time.** VS1 inclusions are small, minor, and positioned in ways that do not amplify visibly in step facets. If you buy VS1 in asscher, you do not need to request photos. The clarity works.

**VS2 — Eye clean roughly 70–80% of the time.** The remaining 20–30% have inclusions that, while graded VS2, sit in positions where the Mirror Multiplication Effect makes them visible face-up. Always request magnified overhead photos before buying VS2 asscher.

**SI1 — Eye clean roughly 20–30% of the time.** The small minority of SI1 asscher stones that pass eye-clean inspection tend to have inclusions confined to the very edge, near the girdle, or under the bezel facets. They require expert photo review and ideally a video. Never buy SI1 asscher sight unseen.

**VVS1/VVS2 — Eye clean 100% of the time.** The inclusions in VVS2 cannot be seen under 10× magnification in most cases, let alone by the naked eye. The premium over VS1 is real but buys nothing visible. You are paying for the certificate number, not the stone's appearance.

The practical takeaway: in asscher cut diamonds, VS1 is the first grade where you stop worrying. VS2 is the grade where you need homework. SI1 is the grade where most buyers who try it regret it.

---

## Asscher Cut Diamond VS1 vs VS2 — Which Should You Buy?

This is the most common clarity question asscher buyers face. The answer depends on how much verification you are willing to do.

**Buy VS1 if:** You are shopping online, you want certainty without reviewing individual stone photos, you are at 2ct or above, or you are buying as a gift where you cannot easily return and verify.

**Buy VS2 if:** You can review high-resolution overhead photos and a loupe video of the specific stone before buying. You are shopping at 1ct or under. You are willing to pass on a VS2 stone that shows any visible inclusion in the photos. The savings justify the extra diligence.

At 1ct, the price difference between VS2 and VS1 in natural GIA-certified asscher runs roughly $200–$500 depending on color grade. At 2ct, the gap grows to $1,500–$4,000. At 1ct, the savings from VS2 are modest enough that most buyers are better off taking VS1 and not worrying.

At 2ct, the VS2 route becomes worth considering if you are comfortable doing photo reviews.

**The rule: never buy VS2 asscher without photos. Never buy SI1 asscher without a video and expert review.** These are not preferences — they are the practical consequences of step-cut optics.

A VS2 asscher that passes photo review is a perfectly good stone. A VS2 asscher bought blind is a coin toss.

