## The Clarity Floor Rule

Here is the framework that determines the minimum clarity grade for any asscher purchase. Call it the Clarity Floor Rule.

The floor changes with carat weight because the step facets grow in size as the stone grows. Larger facets mean longer light corridors, which mean inclusions travel further before reflecting back — increasing their visibility. A VS2 inclusion that is borderline-visible in a 1ct asscher is clearly visible in the same grade at 2ct.

**The Clarity Floor Rule by size:**

| Stone Size | Clarity Floor | Why | Recommended Grade |
|---|---|---|---|
| Under 1.00ct | VS2 (with photos) | Smaller facets — Mirror Multiplication is less extreme | VS1 (skip photo review) |
| 1.00ct–1.49ct | VS2 (with photos) | Standard asscher proportions — VS2 can pass if positioned well | VS1 (no compromise) |
| 1.50ct–2.00ct | VS1 | Facets large enough to amplify all VS2 inclusions | VS1 minimum, VVS2 if budget allows |
| 2.01ct–3.00ct | VS1 | Step facets expose mid-table inclusions clearly | VS1 strict; VVS2 recommended |
| 3.00ct+ | VVS2 | Large corridors amplify every flaw — even some VS1 positions show | VVS2 or higher |

**What this means:** The Clarity Floor Rule moves up by one clarity grade approximately every 1.5ct. At 1ct, you can make VS2 work with homework. At 2ct, VS2 is a risk.

At 3ct+, VS1 becomes the ceiling and VVS2 becomes the practical floor.

**Verdict:** Apply the Clarity Floor Rule before you set your budget. Decide your target size first, then set clarity based on the floor for that size. Do not decide on clarity without knowing the carat weight — the two are inseparable in asscher.

---

## What Inclusions to Avoid in Asscher Cut Diamonds

Not all inclusions are equal in asscher. The type and position of an inclusion determines how visible it becomes through the Mirror Multiplication Effect.

**Inclusions to avoid — high risk in asscher:**

**Crystals at the table center.** A crystal inclusion positioned directly under the table facet sits in the most exposed position in the stone. It will be visible face-up and may be reflected in 2–4 additional facets. Any GIA report noting a crystal "under table" or "at center" should be treated as a visible inclusion regardless of grade.

**Clouds in the top half of the stone.** A cloud is a group of microscopic pinpoints that individually are undetectable but together create a hazy area. In brilliant cuts, clouds scatter with the surrounding sparkle.

In asscher, a cloud in the upper half of the stone creates visible haziness in the mirrored corridors. A GIA comment of "clarity grade based on clouds not shown" is a warning — the clouds are too numerous to plot individually, which means they are significant.

**Feathers near the girdle at VS2 or below.** A feather is a small fracture. In an asscher, a feather near the thin girdle can catch light and appear as a bright white line visible face-up.

It also raises durability concerns at the girdle edge. VS2 feathers near the girdle should be reviewed carefully in both overhead photos and a tilt video.

**Inclusions to accept — lower risk in asscher:**

**Pinpoints below VS1.** A single pinpoint at VS1 is invisible in all viewing conditions. Pinpoints at VS2, unless clustered, typically remain below visibility in asscher.

**Needles parallel to facet edges.** A needle inclusion running parallel to a step facet is less likely to catch light directly than one running across the facet. GIA reports noting "needle parallel to facet edge" in VS2 are generally safer selections.

**Inclusions near the girdle under the bezel.** The bezel facets of an asscher run at a steep angle near the girdle. Inclusions in this region are partly shielded from the main light corridors and are less likely to multiply through the step facets.

---

## Minimum Clarity for Asscher Cut: Practical Buying Rules

Five rules to apply before every asscher clarity purchase:

**Rule 1: VS1 is the default.** If you are shopping online without photo access, or if you are uncomfortable reviewing diamond photos, VS1 is your grade. It works every time without any additional verification.

**Rule 2: VS2 is a conditional yes.** VS2 can work but only when you have reviewed overhead photos of the specific stone in question and the inclusion is confirmed away from the table center. If the retailer cannot provide photos, treat VS2 as SI1.

**Rule 3: SI1 is a conditional no.** The only asscher SI1 stones worth considering have inclusions confined to the outer edge under the bezel, confirmed by a video review showing the stone face-up in normal lighting. Even then, fewer than 30% of SI1 asschers pass this standard.

**Rule 4: Size up your clarity as you size up your stone.** When you move from 1ct to 2ct, your clarity floor moves too. Do not keep VS2 when moving to 2ct because you "budgeted for VS2" at 1ct. The 2ct step facets are larger — they will show what the 1ct facets would have hidden.

**Rule 5: GIA comments matter more than the grade in asscher.** A VS2 stone with the GIA comment "crystal near girdle" is safer than a VS2 with "crystal at center table." The grade tells you how the inclusion affects transparency. The comment tells you where it sits — and in asscher, position determines visibility more than grade.

