## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the best clarity for an asscher cut diamond?

VS1 is the best practical clarity grade for most asscher cut buyers. It is eye-clean in 99%+ of stones without requiring photo verification. VS2 can work but requires individual stone photo review.

VVS2 and above provide no visible benefit over VS1 but cost significantly more.

### Is VS2 eye clean in asscher cut diamonds?

VS2 is eye-clean in approximately 70–80% of asscher cut diamonds. The remaining 20–30% have inclusions in positions where the step-cut facets make them visible face-up. Always request overhead magnified photos before buying VS2 in asscher — never buy VS2 asscher sight unseen.

### Is SI1 ever acceptable in asscher cut diamonds?

SI1 is eye-clean in only about 20–30% of asscher cut diamonds, compared to 60–70% of round brilliants. The step facets amplify and multiply inclusions. SI1 can be considered only with expert photo and video review confirming the inclusion is at the outer edge under the bezel facets — never at the table center.

### Why does the asscher cut show inclusions more than round brilliant?

The asscher's flat, parallel step facets create long, clean light corridors that reflect like mirrors. A round brilliant's kite-shaped and triangular facets scatter light in thousands of directions, hiding inclusions in the visual movement. The asscher has no such cover — inclusions sit inside still mirrors and are visible without interference.

### What clarity should I buy for a 2 carat asscher cut diamond?

VS1 is the minimum for a 2 carat asscher cut diamond. At 2ct, the step facets are larger than at 1ct, which means inclusions travel further through the corridors before reflecting — increasing visibility. VS2 at 2ct is a meaningful risk even with photo review.

F-VS1 at [$21,213](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27103709?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the recommended starting point.

### What clarity should I buy for a 1 carat asscher cut diamond?

VS1 is the recommended grade at 1ct. E-VS1 at [$2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the benchmark. VS2 at 1ct is a conditional option if you review overhead photos of the specific stone.

Never buy SI1 at 1ct in asscher without video review and expert confirmation.

### Does VS1 in asscher need photo verification?

No. VS1 is the grade where you no longer need to verify the specific stone. VS1 inclusions in asscher are small, positioned away from the main light corridors, and not visible face-up in any lighting condition.

VS2 requires photos. VS1 does not.

### What inclusions are worst in asscher cut diamonds?

The most dangerous inclusions in asscher are crystals at the table center, clouds in the upper pavilion, and feathers near the girdle. These positions align with the main light corridors in the step-cut geometry and are amplified by the Mirror Multiplication Effect. Inclusions near the girdle under the bezel facets are the safest positions.

### Is VVS2 worth buying over VS1 in asscher?

No, not for visual reasons. VVS2 and VS1 are both eye-clean in all asscher cut diamonds. The difference is only visible under 10× magnification.

The premium for VVS2 over VS1 in asscher ranges from $300–$2,000+ depending on carat weight — that budget is better spent on size. Buy VS1 and spend the VVS2 premium on carat weight.

### What does GIA clarity mean for asscher cut diamonds?

GIA uses the same 11-grade clarity scale (FL through I2) for asscher as for all other shapes. What differs is how each grade performs visually. A GIA VS2 asscher and a GIA VS2 round have the same certificate grade but very different face-up clarity.

The asscher VS2 requires photo review; the round VS2 typically does not. Always read the GIA comments section — not just the grade — when buying asscher.

### How do I check if an asscher cut diamond is eye clean?

Request an overhead face-up photo taken from directly above in normal daylight-equivalent lighting, not in the exaggerated overhead lamps used for beauty shots. Also request a loupe video showing the stone being tilted slowly under natural light. If the retailer cannot provide these, do not buy below VS1.

If they can, look for any dark or white marks visible in the table area before committing.

### Does lab grown asscher cut diamond clarity matter as much as natural?

Yes, exactly as much. The optical physics of step-cut facets apply equally to lab and natural diamonds — the crystal structure and optical properties are identical. The Mirror Multiplication Effect does not distinguish between lab and natural origin.

VS1 minimum applies to lab asscher the same as natural asscher. The difference is that lab pricing makes VS1 and IF grades far more accessible — see the [lab grown asscher guide](/lab-grown-asscher-cut-diamond) for full pricing.

### What is the minimum clarity for asscher cut diamond at 3ct?

At 3ct and above, VVS2 is the recommended minimum for asscher cut diamonds. The large step facets on a 3ct stone amplify inclusions more than at smaller sizes. VS1 at 3ct can pass but requires careful stone selection.

The safest natural entry at 3ct is [F-VVS2 at $36,991](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28507345?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab [D-IF 3ct at $8,849](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26682894?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) eliminates the clarity question entirely.

